<h2>Calculate first-pass clean claim rate</h2><p class="lead">Use aggregate claim counts to measure the share accepted cleanly on first submission.</p><div class="form-grid"><div class="field"><label for="total">Total claims submitted</label><input id="total" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="5000"></div><div class="field"><label for="clean">Claims accepted cleanly on first submission</label><input id="clean" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="4850"></div></div><div class="tool-actions"><button class="btn btn-primary" id="calc">Calculate rate</button></div><div class="result-box"><div class="result-grid"><div class="metric"><span>Clean claim rate</span><strong id="rate">—</strong></div><div class="metric"><span>Claims needing rework</span><strong id="rework">—</strong></div><div class="metric"><span>Rework share</span><strong id="share">—</strong></div></div><div id="warning"></div></div><div class="notice"><strong>Privacy:</strong> Enter aggregate counts only. Do not enter patient names, identifiers, claim numbers or other PHI.</div>
<script>(()=>{const $=i=>document.getElementById(i),n=i=>Math.max(0,Math.floor(parseFloat($(i).value)||0));function go(){const t=n('total'),c=n('clean');if(t<=0||c>t){$('rate').textContent=$('rework').textContent=$('share').textContent='—';$('warning').innerHTML='<div class="notice"><strong class="danger">Check the inputs</strong><br>Total claims must be greater than zero and clean claims cannot exceed total claims.</div>';return}const rw=t-c;$('rate').textContent=(c/t*100).toFixed(2)+'%';$('rework').textContent=rw.toLocaleString();$('share').textContent=(rw/t*100).toFixed(2)+'%';$('warning').innerHTML=''}document.querySelectorAll('input').forEach(x=>x.addEventListener('input',go));$('calc').onclick=go;go()})();</script>
<?php $toolBody=ob_get_clean();ob_start(); ?>
<h2>Clean claim rate formula</h2><p>This calculator uses <strong>claims accepted cleanly on first submission ÷ total claims submitted × 100</strong>. Define “clean” consistently within your organization before comparing periods or teams.</p><h2>How to use the calculator</h2><ol><li>Choose a reporting period and count total claims submitted.</li><li>Count claims accepted for processing without correction or resubmission under your chosen definition.</li><li>Enter aggregate totals only.</li><li>Track the rate alongside the volume and root causes of claims requiring rework.</li></ol><h2>Why the denominator definition matters</h2><p>Organizations can define submitted claims, clearinghouse rejections and payer acceptance differently. A rate is only comparable when the operational definition and data source remain stable.</p><h2>Use the metric for process improvement</h2><p>The percentage is most useful when paired with denial and rejection reason analysis, registration quality, eligibility workflows, coding edits, authorization processes and payer-specific trends. Avoid using one aggregate percentage to diagnose a workflow on its own.</p>
